  {
   "k": "1j6w78",
   "en": "The Sultanate of Aussa was a minor west African kingdom with roots back to the formation of the Imamate of Aussa that was formed in 1577 which, after its collapse in 1734, was replaced by the Sultanate of Aussa. The neighboring Ethiopian Empire nominally laid claim to the region but the harsh resistance of the Afar people meant that Aussa remained virtually independent. The Sultanate of Aussa met its end when its rulers agreed to cooperated with ⟦0⟧ during its invasion of ⟦1⟧ in 1935-1936. Aussa was subsequently annexed into Italian East Africa and was later incorporated into the Ethiopian Empire after World War II but was able to retain a considerable degree of autonomy until the rise of the Derg in the 1970s. After the fall of the Derg regime in 1991, the Aussa monarchy was reinstated and remains in place today within modern Ethiopia.",
